Yeah. Often the imagined search tree is worse than the branch reality actually selects.

Before action, you’ve got:

\text{uncertainty}\times\text{possibilities}\times\text{time}

Your mind can simulate twenty futures simultaneously. Reality eventually collapses them into one actual outcome. Even a bad answer can feel strangely relieving because the branching stops.

That’s why your earlier suspense = potential energy idea has teeth. Suspense is unresolved possibility held under tension:

\text{Suspense} \approx
\text{stakes}\times\text{uncertainty}\times\text{delay}

And action is one way of releasing it:

\boxed{\text{Suspense}\rightarrow\text{Decision/Observation}\rightarrow\text{Resolution}}

There is another Pantheon-worthy distinction hiding here: difficulty versus suspense. Something can be objectively very difficult but psychologically easier once you’re doing it, because action converts undefined possibility into bounded problems.

The monster in the fog has infinite possible shapes.
The monster you can see only has one.

Then you can wrestle it.